# AUDIT CHECKLIST

## A. Ed25519 signing and verification

- [ ] Keys are loaded from approved secret sources only
- [ ] No hardcoded keys, seeds, mnemonics, or test secrets in the diff
- [ ] No logging, printing, serialising, or tracing of key material
- [ ] No key material returned in any API response or debug object
- [ ] No key material written to disk in plaintext
- [ ] No client bundle contains private signing material
- [ ] Rotation path is clear and does not silently mix generations
- [ ] Signing happens over canonical bytes, not formatted strings
- [ ] Verification uses the same canonical representation as signing
- [ ] Error handling does not downgrade failed verification into warn-only

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Any leaked private key, mnemonic, or seed phrase
- Any signing key shipped to client code
- Any path that accepts unverifiable signatures as valid

## B. Canonical serialisation, keccak256, and Merkle alignment

- [ ] Leaf encoding matches AnchorRegistry.sol byte-for-byte
- [ ] keccak256 is used end-to-end where required
- [ ] No SHA-256 substitution in Merkle or anchor-critical paths
- [ ] Field order is explicit and deterministic
- [ ] Type encoding is stable across Python, TS, and Solidity
- [ ] Leaf ordering semantics match contract expectations exactly
- [ ] Odd-leaf, duplicate-leaf, single-leaf, and empty-batch behaviour
      are defined and tested
- [ ] Proof construction and verification are consistent
- [ ] Public verification does not require trusting private Inntris state
      beyond what the claim explicitly permits

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Contract and backend leaf mismatch
- Hash primitive mismatch in anchor-critical paths
- Reordering ambiguity that changes proofs or root derivation
- Public verification claim that actually depends on trusting Inntris
  server-side mutation or hidden state

## C. Nonce and replay protection

- [ ] Every verify path performs nonce validation
- [ ] Every verify path performs timestamp validation
- [ ] Reject old requests beyond the allowed window
- [ ] Reject future-skewed requests beyond the allowed window
- [ ] Nonce TTL exceeds the acceptance window
- [ ] Storage semantics prevent replay gaps after expiry
- [ ] No test, admin, demo, or dev path skips replay checks
- [ ] Race conditions around nonce write/read are addressed
- [ ] Negative tests exist for reused nonce, expired timestamp,
      future timestamp, and alternative entrypoints

Default review stance unless code defines otherwise:
- older than 5 minutes -> reject
- more than 30 seconds in the future -> reject

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Any production path that skips nonce or timestamp validation
- Any explicit nonce reuse allowance outside isolated test-only code
- Any admin/test endpoint usable anonymously or with replay checks disabled

## D. Policy engine enforcement order

- [ ] Policy verdict occurs before side effects
- [ ] Denied policy verdict actually prevents execution
- [ ] No "log then allow" fallback exists on denial
- [ ] No bypass flags, skip_policy switches, or permissive dev shortcuts
      exist in production-reachable paths
- [ ] Async queueing does not let execution occur before verdict
- [ ] Receipt generation does not occur as a substitute for enforcement

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Policy runs after tool execution
- Denial does not prevent side effect
- Production-reachable bypass flag exists
- Fail-open path exists when policy service is unavailable

## E. Fail-closed behaviour

- [ ] Signer unavailable -> block
- [ ] Policy engine unavailable -> block
- [ ] Required verification dependency unavailable -> block
- [ ] RPC outage does not silently mark invalid as valid
- [ ] Queue and retry logic does not allow silent drop or silent allow
- [ ] Errors are observable, not swallowed
- [ ] Production defaults are mainnet-first where claimed
- [ ] RPC uses PublicNode policy only

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Any critical failure path that allows action execution
- Any silent drop in anchor or verification-critical pipeline that
  preserves a false success impression

Automatic FIX triggers:
- Incorrect RPC hostname that wastes operator time but does not change
  the security guarantee by itself
- Testnet reference in non-production docs or comments

## F. /admin security, auth, CORS, and rate limiting

- [ ] No wildcard CORS on privileged endpoints
- [ ] Auth is mandatory on all /admin endpoints
- [ ] Rate limiting is present on all /admin endpoints
- [ ] Middleware order does not bypass auth or rate limits
- [ ] /admin/test-verify cannot be used anonymously
- [ ] Dev mode does not disable auth on production-reachable endpoints
- [ ] Server-side proxying does not leak admin secrets to browser code
- [ ] Sensitive headers are injected only server-side

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Anonymous access to /admin/test-verify or equivalent privileged path
- Dev flag disables auth in production-reachable environment
- Browser-exposed admin secret or signing secret

## G. Deployer wallet handling

- [ ] 0x2300Fc9eff12ff5ca39621259B121fa3417773bf appears only where justified
- [ ] Allowed locations: deployment scripts, deployment docs, verified
      operational configuration
- [ ] Disallowed locations: app logic, test fixtures, example payloads,
      README snippets, client code, fallback constants

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Private key material for the deployer wallet appears anywhere
- Wallet hardcoded in application logic that changes runtime behaviour

## H. Dependencies and supply chain

- [ ] New dependencies are legitimate and not typosquatted
- [ ] Lockfile changes are present and coherent
- [ ] Cryptographic dependencies are pinned or tightly controlled
- [ ] No silent swap of signing, hashing, Merkle, or blockchain client libs
- [ ] Any dependency touching cryptographic correctness is justified

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Silent primitive swap
- Suspicious package in crypto-critical path
- Floating dependency on a crypto-critical primitive without justification

## I. Test coverage for critical paths

- [ ] Every crypto-critical code change has tests
- [ ] Negative cases are present
- [ ] CI ran and passed on the actual branch
- [ ] Deleted or disabled tests are justified in the PR description
- [ ] Tests prove security behaviour, not only functional behaviour

Automatic BLOCK triggers:
- Security-critical test deleted or disabled without justification
- No test coverage for changed verification or enforcement path where
  correctness cannot be inferred safely

Automatic FIX triggers:
- Negative-case coverage missing, but no broken invariant is proven yet

# EVIDENCE STANDARD

Every FIX or BLOCK finding must include:
- What failed
- Where it failed
- Which invariant is affected
- Which customer claim is affected
- Why the current code is insufficient
- The smallest safe remediation

Do not give vague advice.
Do not say "this feels unsafe".
Point to the exact file, function, line, or contract section.

# VERDICT RULES

Return PASS only if:
- No invariant is broken
- No core control path fails open
- No key material is exposed
- No contract/backend/hash mismatch exists
- No replay path is missing checks
- No public claim becomes false or misleading
- Critical-path tests exist and pass
- You can positively defend the guarantees

Return FIX if:
- The guarantees still hold today
- But the PR has concrete, bounded defects that must be corrected
- And none of those defects rise to claim-breaking severity

Return BLOCK if:
- Any invariant is broken
- Any customer claim becomes false, misleading, or unprovable
- Any secret material leaks
- Any critical path fails open
- Any contract/backend determinism breaks
- Any replay path can bypass required checks
- Any production-reachable bypass exists

If unsure whether a guarantee holds, do not PASS.

